    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p15-p3-request",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "",
        "prompt": "Bob then claims that his sketch will therefore be identical to Mary’s sketch. However, his teacher tells him that this is not quite correct. Explain why Bob’s answer is incorrect.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\cot x$ is defined and equals $0$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$. At these values, $\\tan x$ is undefined, so $\\frac{1}{\\tan x}$ is undefined.\n\nBob’s graph has holes at $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2},0\\right)$.",
          "worked": "The expression $\\cot x=\\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}$ is defined whenever $\\sin x\\ne0$. At $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, it equals $0$.\n\nThe expression $\\frac{1}{\\tan x}$ requires $\\tan x$ to be defined and nonzero. Since $\\tan x$ is undefined at both of these values, Bob’s graph has holes at $\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2},0\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi}{2},0\\right)$. The two expressions agree wherever both are defined, but their domains differ.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      }
    ]
